{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>If YHWH your Elohim enlarges your border</b>. This passage is future-oriented, and I have already addressed this matter in the section of <i>Re'eh Anokhi</i> (above, Deuteronomy 12:20). For at first He said, \"When YHWH your Elohim cuts off the nations whose land YHWH your Elohim is giving you,\" and the expression \"the nations whose land YHWH your Elohim is giving you\" alludes to all seven, as He mentions everywhere. Likewise He says of them, \"and He will thrust out these nations from before you\" (Deuteronomy 7:22), and \"because of the wickedness of these nations\" (Deuteronomy 9:4), and \"YHWH will dispossess all these nations from before you\" (Deuteronomy 11:23), \"only from the cities of these peoples\" (Deuteronomy 20:16), and similarly in many places. And He commanded that after the inheritance and settlement in their land, they shall set apart three cities, and with them the commandment is fulfilled, as He said, \"Six cities of refuge shall they be for you\" (Numbers 35:13), and He said, \"the three cities you shall give across the Jordan, and the three cities you shall give in the land of Canaan\" (Numbers 35:14), and the Torah did not command more than these six cities. And so too all the commandments in the Torah that apply after inheritance and settlement — they become obligated in them after the inheritance of these seven nations. But in this commandment alone He added here, \"If YHWH your Elohim enlarges your border,\" and He did not say <b>as He spoke to you</b>, as He said in the case of \"When YHWH your Elohom enlarges your border\" regarding meat of desire (above, Deuteronomy 12:20). For YHWH did not promise them anywhere to give them only these seven nations, but here He mentioned the patriarchs: \"If YHWH your Elohim enlarges your border, as He swore to your fathers, and gives you all the land that He spoke of giving to your fathers\" — and this alludes to all ten nations that were spoken of to Abraham. And He made this conditional: \"if you keep all the commandment,\" etc.\n\nNow it is a clear matter that even the seven nations themselves they would not inherit if they transgressed the Torah, as He said many times: \"that you may live, and go in and possess the land\" (Deuteronomy 4:1), \"in order that you may enter the land\" (Deuteronomy 27:3), \"for if you diligently keep all this commandment, then YHWH will dispossess all these nations from before you\" (above, Deuteronomy 11:22–23). And when Achan transgressed the ban, and he was but one man, it was said, \"and the children of Israel could not stand before their enemies; they turned their backs before their enemies\" (Joshua 7:12). If so, what is this condition that He makes regarding these three nations alone — \"if YHWH your Elohim enlarges your border, if you keep all the commandment,\" etc.?\n\nThe reason for this is that it was the will before Him, blessed be He, to give them as an inheritance in the days of Moses and Joshua the seven that He promised them in Egypt and in the wilderness, for it was revealed before Him that their merit would attain this. Because of this He obligated them in all the commandments upon the inheritance of those, and obligated them in six cities of refuge, and Joshua designated them. But regarding the remaining three, He made it conditional: \"if you keep all the commandment, to love HaShem and to walk in His ways,\" etc. — that is to say, if they fulfill all of them, the entire Torah in its entirety, and no commandment whatsoever is dependent on this other than these three cities. For what reason would it say, \"if you keep, to walk in His ways, all the days — you shall add for yourself three cities\"? For who knows what will be afterward, all the days?\n\nAnd R. Abraham ibn Ezra said (on Deuteronomy 19:9): \"all the days\" — without interruption between them. And this is not correct. Rather, \"if you keep\" — when your merit reaches the point that you keep all the commandment, to love HaShem with a perfect and enduring love, such that it is revealed before Him that you will never sin again forever, then He will enlarge your border and give you all the peoples. And this refers to the days of which He said, \"And YHWH your Elohim will circumcise your heart and the heart of your seed\" (Deuteronomy 30:6). And <b>the heart of your seed</b> alludes to all the days to come, in the manner stated in the Prophets: \"And I will give them one heart and one way, to fear Me all the days, for good to them and to their children after them, and I will make for them an everlasting covenant that I will not turn away from them to do them good, and I will put My fear in their heart so that they do not turn away from Me\" (Jeremiah 32:39–40). And then, when He makes for them an everlasting covenant, they shall set apart these three cities."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:52:21.023706574Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}